# Code generated by Claude
"""Scripted staging source: brings the robot from the spawn state to HANDOFF for the beaker-on-hotplate task.

HANDOFF is the moment the staging has finished (phase DONE) and the arm stage of the task policy takes over the arms.

A placeholder for a future planner: simple, and every task-specific number comes from src/task_config.py (TASK_CFG poses, STAGING_CFG
speeds, ramps and tolerances), none from this file. Units are metres (m), radians (rad), seconds (s) and newtons (N) unless a name says
otherwise (_deg degrees, _mm millimetres, _s seconds).

    source = make_staging_source(model, data, task_cfg=TASK_CFG, cfg=STAGING_CFG, points_fn=framing_points)
    source["step"](obs) -> commands          (the task policy, src/task_policy.py, calls it every tick until the stage is done; obs is
                                              the observation dictionary the control loop builds every tick)
    source["state"]     phase, done, error, phases {name: {start, end}}, arrival, corrections, first_turn_deg, final_turn_deg,
                        planned_final_turn_deg, reapproaches, framing_report
    source["observe"](obs)   its own measurements, called by the task policy on every tick it runs (during staging and after the handoff): base
                             drift per phase, the smallest base-to-bench clearance during DRIVE, the base pose at HANDOFF
    source["report"]()       the staging part of the episode summary (phase durations, arrival, pose corrections, drift, clearance, the pose
                             error at HANDOFF, turn angles, neck framing)
    source["resume"]()       called after an operator had control and hands it back (see resume() inside make_staging_source)
    source["restore_step"](obs, t, dt), source["restoring"]()   put back the pedestal and neck an operator moved, after a hand-back

Phases, in order (one subsystem moves at a time; the neck is exempt and stays at its look-ahead aim while driving):
  DRIVE              turn in place toward the staging (x, y) position, drive straight, turn to the staging yaw, with the ground-truth base pose. Speeds
                     ramp gently (STAGING_CFG). The straight leg follows the line from where it started to the goal (heading plus
                     cross-track correction, so it stays accurate right up to the goal; steering at the bearing to the goal is
                     ill-conditioned near it). Arrival = within drive_pos_tol and drive_yaw_tol with the base at rest for drive_settle_s;
                     the tolerances are never loosened. After drive_max_reapproach corrective re-approaches (or on the timeout)
                     state["error"] is set and the source stops commanding; the runner ends the episode as STAGING_FAILED.
  PEDESTAL           the pedestal command ramps to task_cfg["pedestal_q"].
  CORRECT_PEDESTAL   the pedestal move pushes the base: if it is outside the tolerance, the same go-to-pose controller moves it back (the
                     arms are still tucked), aiming for correction_aim_frac of the tolerance. Skipped (and logged) if the pose is fine.
  ARMS               the task arm moves (minimum jerk, arms_move_s) rest -> ready_via_q -> ready_q, the gripper opening on the way. The targets go
                     through src/rate_limit.py from the arm's applied targets (arm_max_speed), so after an operator has moved the arm
                     and handed back they glide back to the path instead of jumping.
  CORRECT_ARMS       same check. With the arm extended only a creep-speed correction is allowed: a yaw turn in place and a straight move
                     along the heading (forward or back); a sideways error cannot be fixed without a large turn, which would sweep the
                     arm, so it counts as too large. If the needed correction exceeds correction_arms_max_pos / _max_yaw (or the sideways
                     error exceeds the tolerance), or the base-to-bench or arm-to-bench clearance (checked every tick) falls below its
                     minimum, state["error"] is set (STAGING_FAILED): the extended arm is not moved further.
  NECK               frame_points() on points_fn(model, data, task_cfg) (beaker, hotplate, the gripper at its ready pose), the result is commanded,
                     then held.
  DONE               state["done"] = True. The source stops commanding.
Phase end = the target reached, with the moving joints below the settle velocity for the settle time. Every phase has a timeout in
STAGING_CFG (drive_timeout, pedestal_timeout, correction_timeout, arms_timeout, neck_timeout) that does not count the time an operator had
control. state["final_turn_deg"] is the in-place turn done at the staging pose at the end of DRIVE (the staging search's swept clearance
assumed a small one); state["planned_final_turn_deg"] is what the straight approach from the spawn implies. state["corrections"] records,
for each correction step, whether it was needed, the pose error before and after, its duration and (extended arm) the smallest clearances seen.
"""

